---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BlogPostHeader from '../../components/BlogPostHeader.astro';
import { currentMonthYear } from '../../utils/site-date';

const title = "Private Parking Code of Practice: Signage Rules Operators Must Follow | AppealAirportPCN";
const description = "What the Private Parking Code of Practice requires for signs, what to check at an airport drop-off, and how unclear terms may support an appeal.";
const canonical = "https://appealairportpcn.co.uk/guides/bpa-code-of-practice-signage/";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "Private Parking Code of Practice: the signage rules operators must follow",
  "description": description,
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN" },
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN.co.uk" },
  "datePublished": "2026-04-15",
  "dateModified": "2026-10-05"
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} schema={schema}>
  <BlogPostHeader title="Private Parking Code of Practice: the signage rules operators must follow" breadcrumbLabel="Parking signs" breadcrumbHref="/guides/bpa-code-of-practice-signage/" updatedDate={currentMonthYear.toUpperCase()} />
  <main>
    <div class="wrap">
      <article class="blog-post">
        <p>The Private Parking Code of Practice sets minimum sign standards for private parking operators. These rules can be relevant when you challenge an airport PCN.</p>
        <p>Signage is usually one point to raise alongside others. Our <a href="/">appeal templates</a> include it where it may apply.</p>

        <h2>What the Code asks for</h2>
        <p>Section 3 and Annex A cover signs. In general, signs should be:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>At the entrance:</strong> entrance signs must meet clauses 3.1.1 to 3.1.2 and Annex A.</li>
          <li><strong>Clear about the charge:</strong> the parking charge must be shown as required by clause 3.1.3(j).</li>
          <li><strong>Legible:</strong> clause 3.1.4 sets the font requirements.</li>
          <li><strong>Clear about byelaws:</strong> signs must say whether enforcement is under a byelaw or a contract (3.1.3(k)(i)).</li>
          <li><strong>Updated after changes:</strong> temporary notices must remain in place for 4 months after a material change (3.4).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A parking charge is usually claimed under a contract. If important terms were not clearly shown before a driver entered, that may support an argument that the terms were not agreed.</p>

        <h2>What to look for at an airport</h2>
        <h3>Signs appear after the route commits you</h3>
        <p>At <a href="/stansted-drop-off-charge-appeal/">Stansted</a> and <a href="/luton-drop-off-charge-appeal/">Luton</a>, check whether you could see the charge and choose another route before entering the zone.</p>

        <h3>Signs are hard to read</h3>
        <p>Small print, low contrast, poor lighting or signs placed too high or too far away can make terms difficult to read. Note what you could see from the driver's position.</p>

        <h3>The price is not clear</h3>
        <p>Check whether the amount is easy to find on the entry sign. A price hidden in small print or only available through a QR code may be difficult for a driver to see before entering.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Using this in your appeal:</strong> Explain which signs were missing, unclear or too late for you to make a choice. If possible, include your own photos or describe where the signs were. The operator may provide its own evidence in response.
        </div>

        <h2>Why signage can matter</h2>
        <p>If key terms were not clearly brought to the driver's attention, that may affect whether a contract was formed or what terms it included. Raise this point alongside other relevant grounds, such as <a href="/guides/relevant-land-airport-pcns/">the POFA relevant-land rule</a> or <a href="/guides/thornton-v-shoe-lane-parking/">late-notified charges</a>.</p>

        <div class="disclaimer">
          <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> This article is for information only and does not constitute legal advice. For complex cases, consult a solicitor or Citizens Advice.
        </div>

        <details class="sources-section">
          <summary>Sources</summary>
        <ol>
          <li>MHCLG, Private Parking Code of Practice (as laid), clauses 3.1.1 to 3.1.4, 3.1.3(k)(i), 3.4 and Annex A. <strong>Primary.</strong> <a href="https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6ad7343784361ce7947cd7c7/Private_Parking_Code_of_Practice.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GOV.UK PDF</a></li>
          <li>Private Parking Sector Single Code of Practice, the industry code that applied before 21 December 2026. <strong>Primary.</strong> <a href="https://irp.cdn-website.com/262226a6/files/uploaded/sector+single+Code+of+Practice+Version+1.1+130426-2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">IPC-hosted PDF</a></li>
          <li>POPLA, information for MPs (POPLA considers disputes about unclear or inadequate signage). <strong>Primary.</strong> <a href="https://www.popla.co.uk/parliament" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">POPLA</a></li>
          <li><em>ParkingEye Ltd v Beavis</em> [2015] UKSC 67, press summary. <strong>Primary.</strong> <a href="https://mta-sts.supremecourt.uk/cases/docs/uksc-2013-0280-press-summary.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Supreme Court press summary (PDF)</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
